## Routing Table ##
A PowerShell script to get all the net routes for address family IPv4. Each row is read, and the lowest appropriate metric on the next hop and interface is selected. If one of the next hop elements is 0.0.0.0, the destination is directly connected. When there is no more specific route, "0.0.0.0/0" is the default route.


# Task 3 #

## IP Network Design ##

This design describes two switched Ethernet LANs connected to each other via two routers and a 1 Gb/s Ethernet WAN. Networks are used: 80.64.1.0/24 for LAN 1 and 25.93.1.0/24 for LAN 2. All networks use the subnet mask 255.255.255.0.



Simplify the routing: LAN 1 will process local traffic directly, and send all other traffic to 80.64.1.1.  Router 1 has directly connected routes to 80.64.1.0/24 and 10.0.0.0/24, plus a static route to 25.93.1.0/24 via 10.0.0.2. Router 2 has directly connected routes to 25.93.1.0/24 and 10.0.0.0/24, plus a static route to 80.64.1.0/24 via 10.0.0.1. LAN 2 hosts use 25.93.1.1 as their default gateway.




Assuming the IP source is 80.64.1.11, and the IP destination is 25.93.1.11, then for a ping from PC1 to PC4, the IP source would be 25.93.1.11 and the IP destination would be 80.64.1.11. The Ethernet MAC addresses only change at each hop: on the WAN link, the source MAC address is Router 1 WAN and the destination MAC address is Router 2 WAN.

